WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.387
Where examples come from.

2
00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:07.853
Each source has a characteristic strength and a characteristic failure, and good datasets usually mix them.

3
00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:10.224
Real data, labeled by people.

4
00:00:10.504 --> 00:00:16.968
Production inputs (documents your feature actually receives) have the right distribution by definition.

5
00:00:17.248 --> 00:00:19.360
Their labels are the expensive part.

6
00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:29.112
Labelers need a written guideline with worked examples, and you should have two people label a sample independently and measure their agreement (Studio 3, week 4).

7
00:00:29.392 --> 00:00:36.155
Disagreement usually means the guideline is ambiguous, and fixing the guideline is cheaper than fixing the model.

8
00:00:36.435 --> 00:00:37.885
Expert-written examples.

9
00:00:38.165 --> 00:00:42.091
A domain expert writes inputs and ideal outputs from scratch.

10
00:00:42.371 --> 00:00:50.989
These are excellent for rare but important cases (the legal-hold email, the payroll emergency) and for defining the behavior you want.

11
00:00:51.269 --> 00:00:56.709
They drift from reality: experts write cleaner, more typical inputs than real users do.

12
00:00:56.989 --> 00:00:58.163
Synthetic data.

13
00:00:58.443 --> 00:01:01.237
A model generates inputs, outputs, or both.

14
00:01:01.517 --> 00:01:15.491
Self-Instruct (Wang and colleagues, 2022) showed a model can bootstrap instruction data from a small seed set, and Studio 3 used dimension-based generation to cover a space of inputs deliberately.

15
00:01:15.771 --> 00:01:19.056
Synthetic inputs are a fine way to fill coverage gaps.

16
00:01:19.336 --> 00:01:28.232
Their risk is sameness: generated text tends to be more uniform than real text, so check diversity, and keep at least your test set real.

17
00:01:28.512 --> 00:01:29.579
Distillation.

18
00:01:29.859 --> 00:01:37.283
A stronger model labels inputs, and you train a smaller model on those labels: the cost-motivated path from lesson 4.

19
00:01:37.563 --> 00:01:58.939
The research warning here is “The False Promise of Imitating Proprietary LLMs” (Gudibande and colleagues, 2023): models fine-tuned on ChatGPT outputs learned its style convincingly enough to fool crowd raters, but closed little of the gap on factuality and on tasks the imitation data didn’t cover.

20
00:01:59.219 --> 00:02:05.576
For a narrow task like extraction, distillation works much better, provided the teacher’s labels are checked.

21
00:02:05.856 --> 00:02:19.189
Every label a model drafts is a model output, and the checks from Studio 1 apply: verify evidence quotes against the source, check owners and dates, and have a person review a sample (or all of them, for a test set).

22
00:02:19.469 --> 00:02:26.467
The lab’s optional live step does exactly this, and refuses to train on drafts until someone marks them reviewed.
